How this directory was made
---------------------------
A shallow clone of https://github.com/sindresorhus/exit-hook at v5.1.0, then that repository's own test directory and
its fixtures copied here. The only edit is to the import specifiers that reach the library:
each is rewritten to a generated shim, so one unedited suite can grade any implementation.
Everything else — assertions, fixtures, helpers — is upstream's, byte for byte.

Why a clone and not the npm tarball
-----------------------------------
No incumbent ships its tests to npm. Measured 2026-09-13:

    npm pack ansi-escapes && tar tzf ansi-escapes-*.tgz | grep -c test   ->  0

and the same for strip-ansi and for which. A tarball extract could not produce this
directory, so "vendoring" here means a repo clone at the release tag and nothing else.

The shim is not in here
-----------------------
There is deliberately no shim committed beside these tests. `shim.js` — or `shim.mjs`
under a `type: commonjs` package, because the extension has to say the file is ESM — is
written per run by packages/compat-oracle/src/run.ts, since its body names COMPAT_TARGET:
the control run re-exports exit-hook itself, a target run re-exports ours. The
vendor step deletes any shim it finds, so a stale one can never grade the wrong thing.

Reproduce
---------
    npx tsx scripts/vendor-suite.ts exit-hook --version 5.1.0

Every field above is reproduced exactly, with one exception that is a date and not a
defect: `vendored` is the day the copy was taken, so a fresh run writes today's.

The incumbent itself is in here, and that is deliberate
-------------------------------------------------------
`node_modules/exit-hook/` holds the published 5.1.0 tarball, unpacked and committed. It is
five files, MIT, and it exists so that `--control` — the run that grades this suite against
the package that wrote it, and therefore the run that proves the gate before the gate grades
anything of ours — has something to grade. `exit-hook` is in neither the root manifest nor
`package-lock.json`, and PLAN 2.14 settles where a vendored grader's dependency goes: inside
`vendor/<pkg>/`, never into the workspace, because this repository's zero-dependency claim is
about what a *consumer* installs and a vendored grader is neither shipped nor imported.

    npm pack exit-hook@5.1.0
    # sha256  644e471d78302df4c3016bc83103ae2c3c082f9cae0bd2248f97d143b1a5f730
    mkdir -p node_modules/exit-hook
    tar xzf exit-hook-5.1.0.tgz --strip-components=1 -C node_modules/exit-hook

Two things to know about it:

  * It is tracked despite the repository-wide `node_modules` ignore rule, so it was added
    with `git add -f` and a later `git add -A` will not notice changes to it. It is a frozen
    copy of a published release; there is nothing in it to change.
  * `src/vendor.ts` deletes this whole directory before re-vendoring, so a re-vendor of this
    host must put it back. The two commands above are the whole of that.

And one thing it does not yet fix: `run.ts`'s `writeInternalShims()` resolves the host by
bare name from `packages/compat-oracle/dist/` *before* it looks at whether the suite has any
internal imports, so a control run still needs `exit-hook` resolvable from the oracle's own
package as well. See the note on this host in `src/hosts.ts`.

The four signal cases are a race, and it is upstream's clock
-------------------------------------------------------------
`test.js` kills its fixture after a fixed 1000 ms. Measured 2026-09-14 on a machine at load
average 22 across 14 cores, against real `exit-hook`: 4 of 10 fixture runs were killed before
the child had finished evaluating its module graph, so the signal took its default action and
the case failed with `exitCode: undefined`; 0 of 10 failed when the same fixture was killed on
a readiness signal instead. Both the control and the target show it, identically. A 17 / 21 on
this row is that race. The four names are in `src/hosts.ts`.
